<title><p>The Lighthouse Keeper’s Notebook</p></title>

<section id="island">
<title><p>Chapter 1</p><p>The Island</p></title>
<epigraph><p>Count the ships, not the days.</p><text-author>The previous keeper</text-author></epigraph>
<p>The island is a long back of granite, three hundred paces from end to end, with the lighthouse at its northern tip. Nothing grows taller than a sea pink, and the wind never quite stops; on calm days it merely lowers its voice.</p>
<p>My predecessor left me a notebook, a kettle and one piece of advice: <emphasis>count the ships, not the days</emphasis>. I have tried to follow it, with <strong>mixed</strong> success.</p>
<section id="arrival"><title><p>Arrival</p></title>
<p>The supply boat comes every second Thursday when the sea allows, which is to say less often than that. It brings oil, flour, letters and news that is already old.<a l:href="#note1" type="note">[1]</a></p>
<p>The log of the first week is in <a l:href="#log">chapter 3</a>.</p>
</section>
</section>
<section id="light">
<title><p>Chapter 2</p><p>The Light</p></title>
<p>The lamp burns from sunset to sunrise. The beam turns once every twenty seconds: two flashes, a long pause, two flashes.</p>
<image l:href="#seascape.png"/>
<subtitle>A rhyme from the notebook</subtitle>
<poem><stanza><v>Two flashes, then the dark between,</v><v>a count that every pilot knows;</v><v>the lamp is trimmed, the glass is clean,</v><v>and round and round the bright beam goes.</v></stanza></poem>
<empty-line/>
<p><strong>Never</strong> let the clockwork run down. H<sub>2</sub>O freezes in the cistern at 0 °C; the lens turns at 3 <sup>rpm</sup>.</p>
</section>
<section id="logbook">
<title><p>Chapter 3</p><p>The Log</p></title>
<p>These are the first entries of my log, copied out fair.</p>
<table id="log">
<tr><th>Day</th><th>Weather</th><th>Wind</th><th>Ships seen</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monday</td><td>Fog until noon, then clear</td><td>SW 3</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Rain</td><td>W 5</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Clear and cold</td><td>NW 4</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Squalls, supply boat turned back</td><td>NW 7</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Friday</td><td>Overcast</td><td>N 4</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saturday</td><td>Clear, a halo round the moon</td><td>NE 2</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sunday</td><td>Calm; the sea like oil</td><td>variable</td><td>6</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Back to <a l:href="#island">the beginning</a>. Non-ASCII text: Grüße, Καλημέρα, 灯台.</p>
</section>
</body>
<body name="notes">
<title><p>Notes</p></title>
<section id="note1"><title><p>1</p></title><p>The oldest newspaper on the island describes a winter storm that the keeper of the time, by his own account, slept through.</p></section>
